00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:32.180
At the bottom of the world is miles and miles
of desert.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:37.780
Harsh winds blow over gravelly soil, and the
lakes are covered in ice.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:44.740
This is the McMurdo Dry Valleys—4,800 square
kilometers of polar desert situated in the

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:48.339
icy, snowy continent of Antarctica.

00:00:48.339 --> 00:00:53.489
It is surreal and majestic and grand.

00:00:53.489 --> 00:00:59.780
But we are not journeying to Antarctica for
the giant deserts and immense landscapes.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:04.110
We are journeying to Antarctica for this.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:08.350
We’re being very generous to ourselves with
that “we.”

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:15.400
“We” is actually Ariel Waldman, explorer
and president of the San Francisco Microscopical Society.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:22.980
And today, she is our guest master of microscopes
because in 2018, Ariel led a 5 week expedition

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:24.570
to Antarctica.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:29.320
After being awarded a grant from the National
Science Foundation’s Antarctic Artists &amp; Writers

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:33.800
Program, and with additional support from
National Geographic, she hopped on a plane

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:40.010
to New Zealand, then boarded a military transport
aircraft with 185 pounds of gear to get to

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:41.580
McMurdo Station.

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:42.700
Her goal?

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:46.650
To find and study microbes living in Antarctica.

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:50.590
And not to spoil the mystery or anything,
but she found them.

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:55.340
On our journey to the microcosmos, we’ve
met so many incredible organisms that have

00:01:55.340 --> 00:02:01.180
evolved fascinating lives—lives that are
lived in all sorts of strange, strange places.

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:04.620
There’s been ponds, and tanks, and beach
sand.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:08.450
But it’s one thing to imagine microbes being
able to survive anywhere.

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:16.349
It’s another thing to actually find an ostracod
74 feet deep on the seafloor in Antarctica,

00:02:16.349 --> 00:02:20.040
one of the most extreme climates in the world.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:25.269
The organisms that live here are dealing with
frigid temperatures, dark winters, wind, and

00:02:25.269 --> 00:02:26.269
UV radiation.

00:02:26.269 --> 00:02:28.920
So you have to wonder…what survives?

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:33.069
It can’t all be penguins and seals.

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:38.370
Throughout the vast continent is an array
of tiny, thriving creatures carving out their

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:40.650
own lives in the ice.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:46.069
And over the past few decades, scientists
have been finding microbes in ice, exposed

00:02:46.069 --> 00:02:51.409
soils, meltwater ponds, and other pockets
of Antarctic geology.

00:02:51.409 --> 00:02:56.569
With each discovery, scientists understand
a little bit more about how microbes are able

00:02:56.569 --> 00:02:59.700
to survive in this harsh climate.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:06.959
Moreover, the McMurdo Dry Valleys are perhaps
the closest we come to replicating a Martian

00:03:06.959 --> 00:03:13.739
environment on Earth, making it a useful area
for astrobiologists to understand the possibilities

00:03:13.739 --> 00:03:16.709
of life beyond our planet.

00:03:16.709 --> 00:03:22.739
So with five weeks to search and explore the
continent, Ariel set out to find some microbes

00:03:22.739 --> 00:03:24.340
of her own.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:27.749
But how exactly does one find microbes in
Antarctica?

00:03:27.749 --> 00:03:32.519
Well, any microbe hunt is a function of the
environment you are hunting in.

00:03:32.519 --> 00:03:37.950
And for our resident master of microscopes
James, that means tubes, nets, and careful

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.989
monitoring of his freshwater samples.

00:03:40.989 --> 00:03:45.389
For Ariel, it meant finding a way under the
sea ice.

00:03:45.389 --> 00:03:54.909
So, she recruited the help of some divers
who gathered around a hole in the ice and dove in.

00:03:54.909 --> 00:03:58.549
And if you’ve ever wondered what it looks
like underneath an Antarctic ice sheet, Ariel

00:03:58.549 --> 00:04:04.689
ventured down a long metal observation tube
to watch the divers at work and witness the

00:04:04.689 --> 00:04:08.040
hidden life under the ice for herself.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:12.599
After she brought the samples back to her
lab, Ariel used the Antarctic seawater available

00:04:12.599 --> 00:04:17.850
on tap to keep them alive and happy as she
prepared to watch them under the microscope.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:21.180
But Ariel didn’t just rely on divers.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:26.169
She hiked through the McMurdo Dry Valleys,
and ventured to Blood Falls to gather microbes

00:04:26.169 --> 00:04:33.650
living in the subglacial iron oxide that spills
out in bright red colors across the frozen Lake Bonney.

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:40.360
She also hiked up to the top of a glacier
to find natural frozen-over holes on its surface.

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:45.970
These are called cryoconite holes, and they
form because of dark sediment deposits that

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:52.300
melt the ice around them, creating tiny ecosystems
for wandering microbes.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:56.979
And across the surface of the glacier are
these little frozen-over pockets of dirt and

00:04:56.979 --> 00:05:00.460
water that microbes are quite happy to live
in.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:07.420
So with some drills and a little help, Ariel
collected some dirt-filled ice cores to study.

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:13.930
Under the microscope, she found ciliates and
rotifers and tardigrades and cyanobacteria.

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:17.490
And what might be striking to you if you’ve
been following our journey for a while is

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:23.310
that these are microbes that we have seen
before, and quite frequently.

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:25.710
In some cases, this might not be surprising.

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:30.730
After all, tardigrades are well known for
surviving seemingly unsurvivable conditions.

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:37.419
But it is still so remarkable to see a tardigrade
in action—to see its legs wiggling and its

00:05:37.419 --> 00:05:44.990
body curling—and to know that it came from
an icy pocket inside an Antarctic glacier.

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:50.879
And then there are the diatoms, those single-celled
algae encased in their own glass shells.

00:05:50.879 --> 00:05:55.760
We’ve seen their beautiful jewel shapes
before, but these Antarctic varieties can

00:05:55.760 --> 00:06:02.750
sometimes reach such high densities that their
pigments stain the sea ice brown.

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:07.189
Sea ice might seem like a strange place for
these photosynthetic organisms to live.

00:06:07.189 --> 00:06:13.509
But frozen seawater is full of channels and
pores, creating little salty corridors that

00:06:13.509 --> 00:06:20.300
house planktonic creatures (like diatoms)
that get trapped in them as the surface water freezes.

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:22.789
The ice then isn’t just something that freezes.

00:06:22.789 --> 00:06:28.690
It is something that captures, and something
that becomes home.

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:33.419
And the microbes that live in these homes,
whether in the ice or in the water—they

00:06:33.419 --> 00:06:34.810
all have stories to tell.

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:40.659
In the beginning of the 20th century, Captain
Robert Falcon Scott led the Discovery Expedition

00:06:40.659 --> 00:06:42.050
through Antarctica.

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:48.100
And among the bits of Antarctica the expedition
brought back were mats of cyanobacteria collected

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:51.039
from ponds of melted water.

00:06:51.039 --> 00:06:55.909
These samples were carefully preserved and
stored in London’s National History Museum,

00:06:55.909 --> 00:07:00.940
allowing scientists more than a century later
to study their composition and compare them

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:05.470
to the populations of cyanobacteria that exist
in Antarctica now.

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:10.470
They found that the populations had stayed
roughly the same—that the cyanobacteria

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:15.189
we see now are similar to the cyanobacteria
that existed back then.

00:07:15.189 --> 00:07:21.610
To some degree, this is a kind of comfort
in an era where so much of our ecologies are shifting.

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:27.530
But it may also be that the time we have spent
in Antarctica is too short, and that the cyanobacteria’s

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:31.340
future will look quite different from its
present.

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:36.580
After all, the cyanobacteria and other microbes
that are in Antarctica now may look similar

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:42.150
to organisms we see outside of the frozen
continent, but they are still uniquely adapted

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:46.180
to a very particular and harsh way of life.

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:51.400
So the consistency we see over the past century
may just be a function of the fact that these

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:58.810
organisms are so well-suited to their environment
that new organisms have difficulty finding their way in.

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:05.889
But in a changing world, those well-carved
niches may shift, changing the landscape and

00:08:05.889 --> 00:08:07.780
the life within it.

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:13.590
Because Antarctica, for all its appearance
of a barren land of ice and snow, is not so

00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:15.120
barren after all.

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:21.170
It is full of life, found in different corners
and unusual pockets.

00:08:21.170 --> 00:08:23.960
And life shifts and changes.

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:29.599
But whatever these microbes tell us of our
world and our time—or perhaps even a distant

00:08:29.599 --> 00:08:37.510
world and a distant time—they are a reminder
that even in deeply unfamiliar lands are deeply

00:08:37.510 --> 00:08:40.070
familiar friends.

00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:49.690
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

